waxseal keeps the plaintext of every Kubernetes secret in Google Secret
Manager and only ciphertext in Git. Metadata under .waxseal/ pins each key
to a GSM secret and version, so manifests can be re-sealed at any time.

Every command can be driven entirely by flags. On a terminal, waxseal
prompts for whatever you leave out; with --no-input or in CI it fails
instead, naming the missing flag.

Usage:
  waxseal [command]

Secrets and keys:
  key         Add keys, set their values and change how they are managed
  secret      List, inspect and retire secrets

Operations:
  check       Check certificate, expiry, metadata, GSM and cluster state
  reminders   Expiry reminders in Google Tasks or Calendar
  reseal      Re-encrypt manifests from Secret Manager
  rotate      Generate new values for generated keys and reseal them

Setup:
  cert        Manage the controller certificate
  completion  Generate the autocompletion script for the specified shell
  discover    Find SealedSecret manifests in the repository
  gcp         Provision GCP for waxseal
  help        Help about any command
  import      Register manifests by reading their values from the cluster
  init        Write .waxseal/config.yaml for this repository
  setup       Guided first-time setup (interactive)

Flags:
      --dry-run         show what would change without changing anything
  -h, --help            help for waxseal
      --no-color        disable colour
      --no-input        never prompt; fail if an input is missing
  -o, --output string   output format: text or json (default "text")
      --repo string     path to the repository (default ".")
      --verbose         log subprocess calls and debug detail to stderr
  -v, --version         version for waxseal
  -y, --yes             answer yes to confirmations

Use "waxseal [command] --help" for more information about a command.
